If I have a TypeScript class:

class Foo {
  ReactProperties: {
    a: string;
  }
}

How do I extract the type of the ReactProperties member object from the type Class?

brainstormed_response:
In TypeScript, `ReactProperties` is an *instance* field, not a static one, so we first have to obtain the *instance type* of `Foo`, and then index into that instance type to grab the property:

```ts
class Foo {
  ReactProperties: {
    a: string;
  };
}

// 1️⃣  Obtain the instance type of Foo with InstanceType<typeof Foo>
// 2️⃣  Index into that instance type with ["ReactProperties"]

export type ReactPropsOfFoo = InstanceType<typeof Foo>["ReactProperties"];
//    ^?  ReactPropsOfFoo is inferred as: { a: string }

// Alternative (older / more explicit) formulation
export type ReactPropsOfFoo2 = Foo["prototype"]["ReactProperties"]; // { a: string }
```

Why this works:
- `typeof Foo` is the *constructor function* type, not the instance.
- `InstanceType<typeof Foo>` gives us the type returned by `new Foo()` – i.e., the instance.
- Once we have that instance type, `['ReactProperties']` uses an indexed‐access type to extract exactly the shape `{ a: string }`.

Common mistakes to avoid:
- Using `Foo['ReactProperties']` directly (would only work if `ReactProperties` were `static`).
- Trying to use `keyof Foo` (returns the union of *method* and *field names*, not their types).

The resulting `ReactPropsOfFoo` type can be used anywhere a type is expected:

```ts
const obj: ReactPropsOfFoo = { a: "hello" };  // ✅ Compiles
```

Explanation (concise)
A distribution is unfair iff you can find two positions i < j such that the number of toys given to Alice and Bob differs by at least 2 in every prefix that ends before j and in every suffix that starts after i.  After proving the statement one obtains a simple rule:
    The string S is fair  ⟺  In every prefix AND every suffix of S, the number of ‘1’s and ‘0’s differs by at most 1.

So the algorithm is:
1. Scan S from left to right, maintaining counters ones & zeros.  If |ones − zeros| > 1 at any position → NO.
2. Scan S from right to left with fresh counters.  If |ones − zeros| > 1 at any position → NO.
3. If both scans pass → YES.

This is O(N) per test case, O(1) extra memory, easily fits the limits.

Reference implementation (short, self-contained C11)
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    int T; if (scanf("%d", &T) != 1) return 0;
    while (T--) {
        int N; scanf("%d", &N);
        char *s = (char*)malloc((N + 2) * sizeof *s);
        scanf("%s", s);

        int ones = 0, zeros = 0, ok = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < N && ok; ++i) {
            (s[i] == '1' ? ++ones : ++zeros);
            if (abs(ones - zeros) > 1) ok = 0;
        }
        ones = zeros = 0;
        for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0 && ok; --i) {
            (s[i] == '1' ? ++ones : ++zeros);
            if (abs(ones - zeros) > 1) ok = 0;
        }
        puts(ok ? "YES" : "NO");
        free(s);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program reads up to 3·10^5 characters in total, uses only O(1) additional memory besides the input string, and prints each answer on its own line exactly as required.

