/* Equivalent, clearer implementation of handleCmdLineArgs()
 * -------------------------------------------------------
 * Parses a command-line string and sets the global flag `IsWindowedMode`
 * according to the following, case-insensitive switches:
 *   /w   –> windowed mode  (IsWindowedMode = 1)
 *   /f   –> full-screen    (IsWindowedMode = 0)
 * While scanning, it respects Shift-JIS multibyte sequences using the
 * helper RK_CheckSJIS(). The logic is identical to the original routine
 * but written in idiomatic, easy-to-follow C.
 */
#include <ctype.h>   /* tolower() */
#include <stddef.h>  /* size_t   */

extern int  RK_CheckSJIS(unsigned int state_and_char);
extern int  IsWindowedMode;          /* defined elsewhere */

int handleCmdLineArgs(const char *args)
{
    unsigned int sjis_state = 0; /* keeps multibyte parsing state */

    for (size_t i = 0; args[i] != '\0'; ++i)
    {
        /* Update Shift-JIS state.  RK_CheckSJIS() returns 1 when the
           CURRENT byte is considered part of a multibyte sequence and
           therefore must NOT be interpreted as an ASCII command switch. */
        if (RK_CheckSJIS((sjis_state & 0xFFFFFF00u) | (unsigned char)args[i]) == 1)
        {
            continue; /* Skip second byte of multibyte char */
        }

        /* Detect command-line switch that starts with '/' */
        if (args[i] == '/' && args[i + 1] != '\0')
        {
            char option = (char)tolower((unsigned char)args[i + 1]);

            switch (option)
            {
                case 'w':   /* "/w" – windowed mode */
                    IsWindowedMode = 1;
                    break;

                case 'f':   /* "/f" – full-screen  */
                    IsWindowedMode = 0;
                    break;

                /* Unrecognised switches are ignored to mimic original code */
                default:
                    break;
            }

            ++i; /* Skip the option letter we just processed */
        }
    }

    return 0; /* Original routine did not return; 0 indicates success */
}

Below is a compact, fully-responsive example that keeps one button hard-left and the other hard-right inside the same parent using Flexbox. Two alternative techniques are included so you can pick the one that best fits your codebase.

---
HTML
```
<!-- Semantic & accessible: real <button> elements inside a wrapper -->
<div class="btn-wrapper">
  <button type="button" class="btn cancel">Cancel</button>
  <button type="button" class="btn primary">Save</button>
</div>
```

CSS (Technique A – simplest)
```css
.btn-wrapper {
  display: flex;               /* activate Flexbox */
  justify-content: space-between; /* pushes 1st item left, last item right */
  align-items: center;         /* vertical centering just in case */
  gap: .5rem;                  /* keeps consistent spacing on wrap */
  flex-wrap: wrap;             /* prevents overflow on tiny screens */
}

.btn-wrapper .btn {
  flex: 0 0 auto;              /* prevent them from stretching */
}
/* OPTIONAL: a mobile fallback that stacks the buttons */
@media (max-width: 400px) {
  .btn-wrapper {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: stretch;
  }
}
```

CSS (Technique B – “margin-left: auto” on the second button)
```css
.btn-wrapper {
  display: flex;
}
.btn-wrapper .btn + .btn {     /* only the second button */
  margin-left: auto;           /* pushes it to the far right */
}
```

Why this works
1. Flexbox lays out items along the main axis. `space-between` takes the free space and evenly distributes it, leaving the first item flush left and the last item flush right—exactly what you wanted.
2. Setting `flex-wrap: wrap` keeps the buttons from overflowing on very small screens; they’ll drop below each other while still looking neat.
3. Technique B is useful if you ever need more than two items and only want the last one to hug the right side.

Common pitfalls (and how we avoided them)
• Forgetting `display:flex` on the parent—without it, `justify-content` does nothing.
• Using `float` or `position:absolute`; both break responsiveness and are unnecessary here.
• Letting the buttons stretch (the default with `flex: 1 1 auto`) so they become huge; setting `flex: 0 0 auto` avoids that.
• Relying on vendor prefixes—Flexbox is fully supported in evergreen browsers now.

Accessibility & semantics
• Real `<button>` elements provide keyboard access and correct semantics out of the box.
• Use clear text labels; if you apply icons instead, add `aria-label`.

