Why the warning appears
-----------------------
Tools such as IntelliJ IDEA, SpotBugs, or Error-Prone warn when a volatile field participates in a *compound* operation (read-modify-write). ``volatile`` guarantees **visibility**, but **not atomicity**.  The expression ``modCount++`` translates to three byte-code steps (load, add 1, store); two threads can interleave these steps and lose an update.

Three possible ways to silence the warning
-----------------------------------------
1. **If the class is single-threaded** → just drop the volatile keyword.
2. **If you only need visibility** → keep ``volatile`` but make every update synchronized, e.g. inside ``synchronized(this)`` blocks.
3. **If you want a lock-free, inexpensive fix** → replace the ``int`` with ``AtomicInteger`` (or ``AtomicIntegerFieldUpdater``).  This is the cleanest minimal change because the code already assumes lock-free access.

Below is option #3 applied.

Full patch (Java 17-compatible)
------------------------------
```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class CircularArrayDeQueue<E> implements DeQueue<E> {
    private E[] array;
    private int front, rear, size;
    private final AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger(); // changed line
    private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 16;

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public CircularArrayDeQueue() {
        array = (E[]) new Object[DEFAULT_CAPACITY];
        front = 0;
        rear = 0;
        size = 0;
        // no need to set modCount, AtomicInteger starts at 0
    }

    public void pushFirst(E elem) {
        expandIfNeeded();
        front = (front - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
        array[front] = elem;
        size++;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();           // changed
    }

    public void pushLast(E elem) {
        expandIfNeeded();
        array[rear] = elem;
        rear = (rear + 1) % array.length;
        size++;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();           // changed
    }

    public E popFirst() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        E elem = array[front];
        array[front] = null;
        front = (front + 1) % array.length;
        size--;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();           // changed
        shrinkIfNeeded();
        return elem;
    }

    public E popLast() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        rear = (rear - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
        E elem = array[rear];
        array[rear] = null;
        size--;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();           // changed
        shrinkIfNeeded();
        return elem;
    }

    /* ... other methods unchanged ... */

    private void resize(int newCapacity) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        E[] newArray = (E[]) new Object[newCapacity];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            newArray[i] = array[(front + i) % array.length];
        }
        array = newArray;
        front = 0;
        rear = size;
        modCount.incrementAndGet();           // changed
    }

    private class DeQueueIterator implements Iterator<E> {
        private int current;
        private final int step;
        private final int expectedModCount;

        DeQueueIterator(int start, int step) {
            this.current = start;
            this.step = step;
            this.expectedModCount = modCount.get(); // changed
        }

        public boolean hasNext() {
            return current != rear;
        }

        public E next() {
            if (modCount.get() != expectedModCount) { // changed
                throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
            }
            E item = array[current];
            current = (current + step + array.length) % array.length;
            return item;
        }
    }
}
```

Why this fixes the warning
--------------------------
* ``AtomicInteger`` performs its updates with a single CAS instruction, so each increment is atomic.
* Reads in the iterator also use ``get()``, ensuring a consistent snapshot.
* We removed the volatile keyword entirely because an ``AtomicInteger`` already provides the same visibility guarantees.

Performance and thread-safety note
----------------------------------
The deque *operations themselves* are still **not** thread-safe; only the modCount tracking is now atomic.  For full thread-safety you would need to surround every state change with the same lock or use a concurrent deque implementation from ``java.util.concurrent``.

Alternative quick fix (single-threaded use case)
-----------------------------------------------
If the deque will never be accessed from multiple threads, the simplest legitimate fix is:
```java
private int modCount; // remove 'volatile'
```
This silences the warning because no visibility guarantee is required.

