Short Answer
-------------
You cannot "make sure that 0x0010 0000 is mapped" from a normal loadable kernel module, because that address is inside the *user* part of the virtual address space.  On every recent x86 Linux kernel, kernel code and data live in the *high* portion of the address space (0xC0 0000 00 on 32-bit, 0xFFFF 8000 0000 0000 on 64-bit).  Instead, ask the kernel for executable memory and use the address it gives back.

Why 0x0010 0000 is unmapped
---------------------------
• x86-32 (no PAE, default config): user 0x0000 0000 – 0xBFFF FFFF, kernel 0xC000 0000 – 0xFFFF FFFF.  0x0010 0000 sits firmly in user space, therefore page tables for that address do *not* exist in kernel context.
• x86-64: user 0x0000 0000 0000 0000 – 0x0000 7FFF FFFF FFFF; kernel 0xFFFF 8000 0000 0000 upward.  Again, 0x0010 0000 is user.
Only a handful of early-boot identity-mapped pages and the fix-map live below PAGE_OFFSET, and a loadable module cannot modify those safely.

Correct way: allocate executable kernel memory
---------------------------------------------
1. Ask the kernel for pages:

   ```c
   #include <linux/module.h>
   #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
   #include <linux/mm.h>
   #include <linux/set_memory.h>

   static void *buf;
   static const unsigned char shellcode[] =
       "\x48\x31\xc0\x48\xff\xc0\xc3"; /* xor rax,rax; inc rax; ret */

   static int __init demo_init(void)
   {
       size_t sz = PAGE_ALIGN(sizeof(shellcode));

       /* 1. allocate writable, non-exec pages */
       buf = vmalloc(sz);
       if (!buf)
           return -ENOMEM;

       /* 2. copy code while pages are RW */
       memcpy(buf, shellcode, sizeof(shellcode));

       /* 3. convert pages to executable, read-only */
       set_memory_x((unsigned long)buf, sz >> PAGE_SHIFT);

       pr_info("shellcode mapped at %p\n", buf);

       /* 4. call it */
       ((void (*)(void))buf)();
       return 0;
   }

   static void __exit demo_exit(void)
   {
       size_t sz = PAGE_ALIGN(sizeof(shellcode));
       set_memory_nx((unsigned long)buf, sz >> PAGE_SHIFT); /* restore NX */
       vfree(buf);
   }
   module_init(demo_init);
   module_exit(demo_exit);
   MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
   ```

   On kernels built with CONFIG_STRICT_MODULE_RWX, `vmalloc` gives writable *and* non-executable memory; `set_memory_x()` flips the PTEs to executable after we finish writing, preserving W^X.

2. Older kernels (< 4.11) that lack `set_memory_x()` can use `module_alloc()` which returns pages already mapped with `PAGE_KERNEL_EXEC`.

Why **not** to force-map 0x0010 0000
------------------------------------
• You’d have to create your own page directory entries with `kernel_set_pte()` or by poking CR3.  This breaks KPTI and crashes the system on the first user access.
• That region might already be used by a user process; stealing it would violate process isolation.
• SMEP/SMAP will make the CPU fault if kernel code jumps into low addresses anyway.

If you *really* need a fixed virtual address (e.g., for a special device), you can use the per-architecture `fix_to_virt()` or `set_fixmap()` helpers, but they only work inside the predefined **fix-map** region (x86-64: 0xffff f580 0000 0000 and up), not at 0x0010 0000.

Bottom line: allocate memory the supported way, mark it executable with `set_memory_x()`, and forget about address 0x100000.

Below is a concise yet thorough walkthrough for “baby’s-first grep” in Rust. Copy–paste segments as you follow along.

──────────────── 1. Create the project ────────────────
$ cargo new rgrep    # short for “Rust grep”
$ cd rgrep

Cargo scaffolds:
  rgrep/
    Cargo.toml   # metadata & deps
    src/main.rs  # entry point

──────────────── 2. Parse CLI args ────────────────
Open src/main.rs and replace the stub with:

use std::{env, fs::File, io::{self, BufRead, BufReader}};
use std::process;

fn main() {
    // Expect pattern and an optional path; default to STDIN
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
    if args.len() < 2 || args.len() > 3 {
        eprintln!("Usage: rgrep <pattern> [file]");
        process::exit(1);
    }
    let pattern = &args[1];

    // Choose input source
    let reader: Box<dyn BufRead> = if args.len() == 3 {
        match File::open(&args[2]) {
            Ok(file) => Box::new(BufReader::new(file)),
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("Could not open {}: {}", &args[2], e);
                process::exit(1);
            }
        }
    } else {
        Box::new(BufReader::new(io::stdin()))
    };

    // Iterate lines and print matches
    for (idx, line) in reader.lines().enumerate() {
        let line = match line {
            Ok(l) => l,
            Err(e) => {
                eprintln!("Read error: {}", e);
                continue; // skip bad line, keep going
            }
        };
        if line.contains(pattern) {
            println!("{:>6}: {}", idx + 1, line);
        }
    }
}

Key points:
• &args[1] gives an &String which coerces to &str for contains().
• Box<dyn BufRead> abstracts over File vs STDIN without generics gymnastics.
• Error handling uses Result and early exits so the compiler is happy about exhaustive cases.

──────────────── 3. Build & run ────────────────
$ cargo run -- "fn main" src/main.rs
     Compiling rgrep v0.1.0 (…)
     Finished dev [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 1.2s
     Running `target/debug/rgrep "fn main" src/main.rs`
     12: fn main() {

STDIN example:
$ cat Cargo.toml | cargo run -- "serde"
      8: serde = "1"

──────────────── 4. Unit test the matcher ────────────────
Add below main():

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*; // bring main scope

    #[test]
    fn finds_substring() {
        let hay = "hello rustaceans";
        assert!(hay.contains("rust"));
    }
}

Run: $ cargo test

──────────────── 5. Next steps (optional) ────────────────
• Switch argument parsing to the “clap” crate for “rgrep –ignore-case –help”.
• Support Regex via the “regex” crate.
• Use iterators & filter_map instead of for-loop.
• Replace Box<dyn BufRead> with generics once comfortable with lifetimes.
• Benchmark vs system grep using `hyperfine`.

──────────────── 6. Rust vs C/C++ quick mapping ────────────────
• &str is like const char* slice; String is heap-owned like std::string.
• `?` operator is sugar for `if(res.is_err()) return Err(e)`.
• No null pointers; Option<T> forces check.
• `use std::process::exit` vs `return EXIT_FAILURE;`.

Have fun hacking—once this compiles you’ve conquered >50% of practical Rust!

Explanation of the error
------------------------
The stack-trace shows that the current thread called `Future#get()` (method `X.0i9.get`) to wait for the result of a background task that had previously been submitted to a thread pool. Between the moment the task was scheduled and the moment `get()` was invoked, that task was **cancelled**, therefore `get()` immediately threw `java.util.concurrent.CancellationException`.

Typical ways a task becomes cancelled
1. Another part of the program explicitly called `future.cancel(true /*or false*/);`  
   • This is common in Android when an `AsyncTask` or custom `Executor` is cancelled in `onStop()`/`onDestroy()`.  
2. A timeout wrapper (e.g., `CompletableFuture[.orTimeout]`, Guava’s `Futures.withTimeout`, RxJava `timeout()` operator) automatically cancelled the underlying task when it exceeded the time limit.  
3. The thread pool itself was shut down (`ExecutorService.shutdownNow()`), which implicitly cancels queued tasks.  
4. A race condition or exception inside the task triggered code that cancels it.

Why your trace looks obfuscated
The package prefix `X` indicates code that has been minified/obfuscated (typical of production Android builds). Map it back with your *mapping.txt* to know the real classes.

How to locate the cancelling call
1. Search the code-base for `cancel(` on the same `Future` variable or the surrounding class.  
2. Temporarily wrap the `Future` in a helper that logs `new Throwable("cancel called")` so you see who cancelled it.  
3. If you use Kotlin coroutines, enable `DebugProbes`—a coroutine that gets `cancel()` logs with a full stack.

Recommended fixes / best practices
----------------------------------
A. If the cancellation is *expected*
   • Catch it and return gracefully:
```java
try {
    return future.get();
} catch (CancellationException e) {
    Log.w(TAG, "Background task was cancelled, falling back to cached value", e);
    return cachedResult;
}
```

B. If the cancellation is *not* intended
   1. Remove or postpone the offending `cancel()` call.  
   2. Replace blanket `shutdownNow()` with `shutdown()` + `awaitTermination()` so running tasks finish.  
   3. Increase time-out values or move long-running I/O to a separate scheduled task that you don’t cancel aggressively.

C. Make the task *cancellation-safe*
   • Frequently check `Thread.interrupted()` inside the task and clean up resources.  
   • Use `CompletableFuture` or Kotlin `Job` hierarchy so you can propagate or ignore cancellation in a controlled way.

End-to-end example of a robust pattern
```java
ExecutorService ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
CompletableFuture<String> downloadFuture = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
    try {
        return downloadBigFile();
    } catch (IOException ioe) {
        throw new CompletionException(ioe);
    }
}, ioPool).orTimeout(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

// caller thread
try {
    String data = downloadFuture.join();
    process(data);
} catch (CompletionException ce) {
    if (ce.getCause() instanceof TimeoutException) {
        // timed out; cancellation intentional
        recoverFromTimeout();
    } else {
        throw ce;
    }
}
```

Debug checklist
---------------
☐ Verify whether the task actually started (set a log at its first line).  
☐ Confirm if `ExecutorService` was shut down earlier.  
☐ Inspect app life-cycle callbacks (`onStop`, `onDestroy`) for `cancel()` calls.  
☐ Enable `-Djdk.internal.httpclient.debug=true` or instrumentation if network timeouts are involved.

Conclusion
----------
`CancellationException` is not a bug in the JDK—it’s a symptom that *your own code or a wrapper library decided to abort the task*. Identify who calls `cancel()`, decide if that is expected, then either catch it and handle gracefully or prevent the unwanted cancellation. Once you control the life-cycle consistently, the exception disappears.

Below is a concise but complete pattern that: (a) provides a *default* mock for the `axios` HTTP client in `setupFilesAfterEnv`, and (b) overrides that mock with a different implementation for one specific test file.

──────────────── jest.config.js ────────────────
module.exports = {
  // …other config
  setupFilesAfterEnv: ["<rootDir>/jest.setup.js"],
};

──────────────── jest.setup.js (runs once *per* test file *after* the test framework is installed) ────────────────
//   1️⃣ Provide a GLOBAL/DEFAULT mock of axios
//      that will be used in *all* tests *unless* a test overrides it.
//   2️⃣ We put the mock here because jest.setup.js is executed *after*
//      hoisted `jest.mock()` calls from each test file, so it acts as a
//      safety-net default.
//   3️⃣ We keep the factory in a separate function so individual tests can
//      re-use it when they want the same behaviour.

function defaultAxiosFactory() {
  return {
    get:  jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({data: {global: true}})),
    post: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({data: {saved: true}})),
    // …other methods you need
  };
}

jest.mock("axios", () => defaultAxiosFactory());

// 👉 Export the factory so that tests can re-use or extend it if they like.
module.exports.defaultAxiosFactory = defaultAxiosFactory;

──────────────── some.spec.js (keeps default mock) ────────────────
import axios from "axios";   // ← receives the default mock defined above
import {myFunction} from "./myFunction"; // code that internally calls axios.get

test("returns global mock data", async () => {
  const result = await myFunction();
  expect(result).toEqual({global: true});
  expect(axios.get).toHaveBeenCalledWith("/endpoint");
});

──────────────── override.spec.js (needs a *different* mock) ────────────────
/**
 * The global mock defined in jest.setup.js runs *after* hoisted mocks in this
 * file, so to override it we use `jest.doMock` *synchronously* before we
 * import the module under test. We must reset modules first to clear the cache.
 */

jest.resetModules(); // 1️⃣ wipe previous module cache so our new mock wins

jest.doMock("axios", () => ({               // 2️⃣ provide alternate behaviour
  get: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve({data: {custom: true}})),
  post: jest.fn(() => Promise.reject(new Error("fail"))),
}));

// Now we can import the module(s) that use axios; they will receive *this* mock.
const {myFunction} = require("./myFunction");
const axios = require("axios");

test("uses custom mock in this file only", async () => {
  const result = await myFunction();
  expect(result).toEqual({custom: true});  // ← custom data
  expect(axios.get).toHaveBeenCalled();
});

──────────────── common pitfalls & notes ────────────────
• Don’t put `jest.mock("axios")` at the *top* of the test file *without* a factory—if you do, Jest hoists it and then the *global* mock in `jest.setup.js` will override it again. Use `doMock` (or `setMock` before `require`ing) combined with `resetModules` so that your local definition is the last one applied.
• Keep factories named so tests can re-use them and stay DRY.
• If you need the *real* module in one test, call `jest.unmock("axios")` after `resetModules()` and before the `require` call, or inside a `jest.isolateModules()` block.
• Always call `jest.resetModules()` when you change mocks at runtime to avoid leakage across tests.

With this pattern you can:
  – Provide a default mock that simplifies 90% of your tests.
  – Override or unmock per file when behaviour differs.
  – Keep mocks isolated and predictable across the suite.

