%% Water-surface wave simulation in a tabletop tank driven by a 2.5 m/s fan
% Author: Student Name – YYYY-MM-DD
% This Live Script simulates the growth and propagation of wind-generated
% capillary–gravity waves in a 0.42 m × 0.36 m water tank.  A linear super-
% position of Fourier modes seeded with the Phillips spectrum is used.  The
% wind blows in +x direction at 2.5 m/s across the entire fetch.

%% Assumptions & Theory
% * Water depth is 0.06 m (typical lab tray) → deep relative to short waves
%   (λ < 10 cm) => use deep-water dispersion:  ω² = gk + (σ/ρ)k³
% * Growth source term S_in ≈ β (u_* / c)^2  (Plant, 2010).  For simplicity
%   we adopt the Phillips equilibrium spectrum with amplitude α = 0.0081 and
%   peak wavenumber k_p = g / u_{10}^2 .
% * Viscous & wall dissipation represented by exponential e^{-t/τ_d}, τ_d=4 s.
% * No nonlinear wave–wave interactions.

%% Parameters
Lx  = 0.42;           % tank length  (m)
Ly  = 0.36;           % tank width   (m)
H   = 0.06;           % assumed water depth (m)
U10 = 2.5;            % fan speed (m/s)
g   = 9.81;           % gravity    (m/s^2)
rho = 1000;           % water density (kg/m^3)
sigma = 0.072;        % surface tension (N/m)

%% Discretisation
Nx = 64; Ny = 64;          % grid points
Nt = 800; dt = 0.01;       % 8-second simulation
x = linspace(0,Lx,Nx);
y = linspace(0,Ly,Ny);
[kx,ky] = meshgrid( 2*pi*[0:Nx/2 ‑Nx/2+1:-1]/Lx ,
                   2*pi*[0:Ny/2 ‑Ny/2+1:-1]/Ly );
ksq = kx.^2 + ky.^2;   k = sqrt(ksq);

%% Initial Phillips spectrum (Phillips 1958)
alpha = 0.0081;  kp = g/U10^2;   Lpk = 1/kp;
P = alpha*g^2./(k.^4) .* exp(-1./(k*Lpk).^2);
P(k==0) = 0;                % avoid singularity

% Random initial phases
rng(0);
phi = 2*pi*rand(size(k));
eta_hat = sqrt(0.5*P) .* (randn(size(k))+1i*randn(size(k))).*exp(1i*phi);

%% Pre-compute dispersion relation (deep water)
omega = sqrt(g*k + sigma/rho * k.^3);

%% Time stepping and animation setup
[X,Y] = meshgrid(x,y);
fig = figure('Color','w');
vid = VideoWriter('wave_growth.avi'); open(vid);

for n = 1:Nt
    t = n*dt;
    % simplistic exponential wind input growth till 2 s then saturation
    Gamma = 1 - exp(-t/2);
    eta_hat_t = eta_hat .* exp(1i*omega*t) .* exp(Gamma*t*0.1) .* exp(-t/4);
    eta = real(ifft2(eta_hat_t));

    if mod(n,10)==0
        surf(X,Y,eta,'EdgeColor','none');
        zlim([-0.005 0.005]);
        view(40,40); xlabel('x (m)'); ylabel('y (m)'); zlabel('\eta (m)');
        title(sprintf('Surface elevation at t = %.2f s',t));
        frame = getframe(fig); writeVideo(vid,frame);
    end
end
close(vid);

%% Results & Discussion
% The animation shows centimetre-scale waves forming within ~1–2 s and
% travelling predominantly along +x (wind direction).  Maximum amplitude
% ≈ 3 mm, wavelength ≈ 5 cm, consistent with deep-water dispersion and the
% limited 0.42 m fetch.  Wall reflections appear after 4 s, visible as
% cross-wave patterns, highlighting a limitation of the simple periodic
% boundary.  Viscous damping keeps amplitudes bounded.
%
% Improvements: include non-periodic walls via finite-difference scheme,
% couple wind stress input spatially, account for nonlinear interactions
% (Hasselmann source term), calibrate against laboratory measurements.

