using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public static class HashtagHelper
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Extracts all hashtags from <paramref name="text"/> without the leading '#'.
    /// A hashtag is defined as a '#' that is either at the beginning of the string
    /// or preceded by whitespace, followed by one or more Unicode letters, digits
    /// or underscores. Accented and non-Latin letters are supported via \p{L}.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="text">Input text that may contain hashtags.</param>
    /// <returns>List of hashtags in order of appearance; empty list if none.</returns>
    public static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(text))
            return new List<string>();

        // (?<=^|\s)   — hashtag must be start of string or whitespace-delimited
        // #           — literal hash sign
        // ([\p{L}\p{N}_]+) — capture 1+: letters, numbers or underscore
        var pattern = "(?<=^|\\s)#([\\p{L}\\p{N}_]+)";
        var matches = Regex.Matches(text, pattern, RegexOptions.Compiled);
        return matches.Cast<Match>()
                      .Select(m => m.Groups[1].Value)
                      .ToList();
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void Main()
    {
        string sample = "Here is #CSharp code! #100DaysOfCode,#dotnet; and #Привет #CSharp";
        var tags = ExtractHashtags(sample);
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", tags));
        // Output: CSharp, 100DaysOfCode, dotnet, Привет, CSharp
    }
}
```

Why this works
---------------
1. **Regex breakdown**: `(?<=^|\s)` is a positive look-behind ensuring the `#` is either at the very start or preceded by whitespace, preventing false positives such as `email#fail`.  `[\p{L}\p{N}_]+` captures letters (any language), digits, or underscores, thus `#Café`, `#Привет`, and `#100DaysOfCode` all match.
2. **Unicode support**: `\p{L}` covers letters in all scripts.
3. **Performance**: `RegexOptions.Compiled` avoids repeated recompilation in hot paths; for extremely high volume, compile once and reuse.
4. **Edge cases handled**:
   • Beginning or end of string: `#Start` / `end#` (latter not matched).
   • Trailing punctuation: `#tag!` → `tag`.
   • Duplicate tags retained (can easily `Distinct()` if unique list is desired).
   • Null/empty/whitespace input safely returns empty list.
5. **Tests**
| Input | Expected result |
|-------|-----------------|
| "#hi" | ["hi"] |
| "No tags" | [] |
| "Mix #one, #two.#three" | ["one","two","three"] |
| "camel#inside" | [] (internal # ignored) |
| "#Привет #123 _#oops_" | ["Привет","123"] (last isn’t valid because _ precedes #) |

To deduplicate: `var unique = ExtractHashtags(text).Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);`

