To measure a shape that sits way down in an SVG hierarchy you can lean on three built-in calls: querySelector, getBBox, and getScreenCTM.

```js
// 1. Grab the nested element (could be 10 levels deep)
const el   = document.querySelector('#mySVG g.deep g.deeper rect');

// 2. Local box (relative to the element’s own coord-system)
const box  = el.getBBox(); // {x, y, width, height}

// 3. Turn that local box into page coordinates
const svg  = el.ownerSVGElement;
const pt   = svg.createSVGPoint();

pt.x = box.x;          pt.y = box.y;
const topLeft     = pt.matrixTransform(el.getScreenCTM());
pt.x = box.x+box.width; pt.y = box.y+box.height;
const bottomRight = pt.matrixTransform(el.getScreenCTM());

const globalBBox = {
  x:      topLeft.x,
  y:      topLeft.y,
  width:  bottomRight.x - topLeft.x,
  height: bottomRight.y - topLeft.y
};
console.log(globalBBox);
```
Why this works
• getBBox() gives the rectangle BEFORE transforms.
• getScreenCTM() stacks every translate/scale/rotate from all parent <g> elements, so matrixTransform() moves the corners into the viewport.
• The same approach survives viewBox changes because CTM already contains that scale.

Need the rectangle in the element’s own space? Skip the matrix step. Need CSS-pixel space? use getBoundingClientRect() on the parent <svg> and add scrollX/scrollY. All in under 250 words!

